<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hrotsvitha of Gandersheim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 935–c. 973</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Hrotsvit, Roswitha of Gandersheim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hrotsvitha of Gandersheim was a canoness of Gandersheim Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> writer, regarded as the first known woman poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the first known dramatist of the Latin West since antiquity. Writing around 960, she composed six plays modelled on those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terence">Terence</a>, verse legends of saints, and an epic in praise of Emperor Otto I, the Gesta Ottonis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hrotsvitha-of-gandersheim.jpg" alt="Hrotsvitha of Gandersheim" width="300" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albrecht Dürer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roswitha_Duerer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
